<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jang Bahadur Rana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Jung Bahadur Rana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jang Bahadur Rana was a Nepalese general and statesman who seized power after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kot-massacre">Kot massacre</a> of 1846 and founded the Rana regime, under which his family held hereditary office as prime ministers while the kings were figureheads. He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1850, issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muluki-ain">Muluki Ain</a> law code in 1854 and sent troops to help the British during the Indian Rebellion of 1857. The Rana regime lasted until 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jang-bahadur-rana.jpg" alt="Jang Bahadur Rana" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dr John William Tyler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jung_Bahadur_Rana_in_1887.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jung Bahadur Rana was born Bir Narsingh Kunwar on 18 June 1817 at Balkot in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, the son of Bal Narsingh Kunwar, a bodyguard of King Rana Bahadur Shah, and Ganesh Kumari, sister of Mathabar Singh Thapa. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhimsen-thapa">Bhimsen Thapa</a> was dismissed in 1837 the family lost its positions and property, and Jung Bahadur worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> and as a mahout before settling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> in 1839. King Rajendra promoted him to captain in 1840, and as a Kaji he learned the state's finances. He won favour with the youngest queen, then the real ruler, and after Mathabar Singh Thapa was assassinated he was promoted general.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>On 14 September 1846 Jung Bahadur and his brothers killed about 40 members of the court, among them Prime Minister Fateh Jung Shah, in the palace armoury, the Kot. The massacre left King Rajendra and Prince Surendra powerless and marked the beginning of Rana autocracy. The queen appointed him prime minister and commander-in-chief on 15 September. When she conspired to have him killed at the garden of Bhandarkhal, he learned of it from his spies, arrived with armed troops and left 23 dead, and the queen went to Benaras.</p><p> In May 1847 he had Rajendra dethroned and Surendra crowned. Rajendra gathered troops at Bettiah, but government forces defeated him in July 1847 at the Battle of Alau and took him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>. By 1850 Jung Bahadur had beaten his main rivals.</p><p>Wanting a direct relationship with the British government, he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> on 15 January and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a> on 15 May 1850. He met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> on 19 June and visited Parliament, and he later saw the president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Neither government agreed to direct relations, but he came away convinced that close cooperation with the British was the best way to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> independent. On his return he set up a body to draft a legal code. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muluki-ain">Muluki Ain</a> was enacted on 6 January 1854. It did not address <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> inequality, and it laid the foundation of modern law in Nepal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jung Bahadur remained prime minister until 1877, suppressing conspiracies and local revolts. Later prime ministers of his family added his name to their own, and the Rana rule is historically known as iron-fisted. Historians differ: some blame him for starting a dark period of oppressive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>, while others blame his nephews, the Shumsher Ranas. His supporters point to his removal of factional fighting at court, innovations in the bureaucracy and judiciary and efforts to modernise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jung Bahadur Rana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nepal also fought Tibet in 1855–1856, and <b>Jang Bahadur Rana</b> sent troops to help the British suppress the Indian rebellion of 1857.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. Died in Borlang, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.71,85.32&amp;year=1856" aria-label="Kathmandu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6789 1148 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jang Bahadur Rana"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7239 1418h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7239 1418h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.71,85.32&amp;year=1856">What was here in 1856? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
